hi in this video i'm going to talk about  gel filtration chromatography or gfc so  gfc is a very important method for  protein purification  so the separation principle is based on  that  separation basis basis is basically on  size and shape of that macromolecule so  protein would be separated on base of  their size  and base of on basis of their shape  so the principle is like that protein  having larger size  would not enter the particular pores of  gel bead used in this chromatography and  protein with smaller size would enter  the gel beads and would be [ __ ] so  protein with higher molecular weight or  protein with greater size  would uh eluted first so it's a column  chromatography so let's see step by step  how this uh gel filtration  chromatography works  so it's a column chromatography as you  can see there is a particular column  and in this column there are a lot of  circular beads  porous beads  and these  porous beads have definite function in  this particular gel filtration  chromatography  and  if we zoom inside these porous beads we  would find that  the small molecules here depicted in  blue can easily get inside the pores of  this bead  and if they get inside the pore of that  bead they would be [ __ ]  in contrast the  big red molecules or red proteins  whatever you might think  these red proteins  would not enter the bead because they  are  too large to get in into the bead  so they would uh  say from the side space they would get  eluted and they would be eluted first  so there is a exclusion limit the  smallest size  by which  the protein or the macromolecule can  enter the bid it's called the exclusion  limit of that particular gel bead  and we would see these exclusion limit  in a moment  and back to our main diagram  so first step would be equilibration  equilibration means we would apply the  buffer and we would  allow the buffer to  run through this  gel to this from this column and after  that second step would be sample  application so we put our sample in our  sample we have our red protein which has  the biggest size then a green protein  and the smallest blue protein so it's a  mixed sample and now we want to  just separate this red green and blue  protein from each other now let's see  what happens after we load the sample  when we load the sample the red proteins  as they are biggest they would  get through these matrix but they won't  enter these gel beads  and  as you can see in this second step  they are getting through these  small pores in the matrix  but they are not entering the gel where  whereas the small blue molecules as they  are small  their size are small  so they can enter these gel beads and  thus they get [ __ ] so that is why  first  the red  proteins or red macromolecules are  eluted then the green one and at the  last the blue ones are eluted so as you  can see in this step wash step we would  be using washing buffer and the red  protein as they have greater size bigger  size  they would be eluted first whereas the  green protein would be eluted next and  at last the blue protein would be eluded  so let's see some popular gel beads  so we have bio gel p that is actually  polyacrylamide based gel and its  exclusion limit is 0.2 to 400 kilodalton  that means  uh if a  protein or a macromolecule which have a  molecular weight  within this limit can enter the gel bead  and we can have also sepharose or bio  gel a  which has  which are agarose based bio gel a that  has the highest exclusion limit so if  you want to purify large proteins then  we would choose these sepharose instead  of bio gel p or cephalics cephadex is  actually dextron based and its exclusion  limit is slightly higher than bio gel p  but a lot more lower than  severals so its uh exclusion limit is  point seven to six hundred kilo dalton  and  now we would talk about  few volumes and how we can predict the  molecular weight from these gel  filtration chromatography  so we have some  volumes here  so total volume would be equal to void  volume plus internal volume plus bed  volume now what is void volume so void  volume are these spaces  so these spaces in uh between two bits  so if we just zoom in this picture  the volume over here i am  shading it in red  would be the void volume  so this is depicted as v  0 or void volume the internal volume is  the volume inside the gel beads  so whatever the volume is inside the gel  bead it is actually internal volume and  the bed volume the total volume occupied  by these gel matrix is actually the bed  volume  so the sum of all volume would give us  the total volume  now  the x we have to understand another  equation that is  volume of illusion that means the amount  of buffer we are giving to elude our  particular protein is actually equal to  void volume plus  sigma times  internal volume  where this sigma is actually partition  coefficient of the particular solute and  here in this case the solute would be  protein  and  this partition coefficient is very  important to calculate the molecular  mass  so if we have say say we have  a lot of non-molecular weight proteins  and  we calculated their  partition coefficient  and what we can do next that we can plot  their partition coefficient and  molecular mass and there would be a  linear relation between their molecular  mass and the partition coefficient and  from that standard curve we can actually  find out  uh the partition coefficient for our  unknown protein and  by which we can actually  use it and extrapolate that so if we  know the partition coefficient for for  our unknown protein and now we can  actually extrapolate that graph to find  the molecular mass of that particular  protein  so in this respect in this  by this method we can actually  predict the molecular rough molecular  mass of that particular protein  and  this is how gel filtration  chromatography is very important so just  to summarize the basic concepts behind  gel filtration chromatography so what we  have in the separation principle we have  the separation is on the basis of size  and shape of our protein and we can  predict the  rough molecular mass of that protein and  other thing we have to remember for this  gel filtration chromatography is that  the proteins that have larger shape  larger size and shape would not enter  the gel beads and would be eluted first  and rapidly  and the proteins which have small size  would enter the jail and thus get  [ __ ] and they would be eluted at  last  so as in our  situation the red protein has  bigger shape so it had it had been  eluted first then the green protein and  at last the blue proteins are eluted  because the blue protein has smaller  size  and that's all about gel filtration  chromatography hope you enjoyed the  video please like and subscribe thank  you
 

